## Goal
Make the live Resonance pilot safe for real user data and agent-mediated actions. This is a dedicated security/privacy gate, not a collection of frontend hiding rules.

The product handles user-generated Thought DNA, coarse location, social discovery, invitations, messages, shared workspaces, and agent tool calls. Those surfaces require explicit server-side isolation and data-lifecycle controls before public launch.

## Data classification / minimization
Classify every persisted/returned field as one of:
- private owner-only;
- shared for structural discovery;
- consented pseudonymous display;
- consented coarse location;
- collaboration-member-only;
- public/system metadata.

Default must be private. Store only data needed for the product. Prefer structured Thought DNA + provenance instead of raw conversation text. Do not persist provider prompts, secrets, access tokens, or unnecessary raw chat history.

## Authorization invariants
- every user-owned object is checked server-side against authenticated subject + role;
- no cross-user access by guessed IDs;
- discovery can return only fields explicitly shareable by candidate consent;
- workspace/channel/artifact/message reads require active membership;
- removing a member/revoking consent immediately revokes access;
- WebMCP, remote MCP, product API, and human UI use the same authorization service/policy;
- never trust user_id/session_id supplied by the browser/agent without authorization lookup.

## Authentication/session security
- secure cookies/tokens; Secure + HttpOnly + SameSite where applicable;
- CSRF protection for cookie-authenticated mutations;
- session rotation on login/privilege changes;
- logout/revocation invalidates credentials;
- no secrets in URLs/logs/repository;
- rate-limit auth/session creation and sensitive write actions.

## Agent/WebMCP/MCP trust boundary
Treat all content from other users as untrusted user-generated content.
- WebMCP tools returning UGC use `untrustedContentHint` where supported;
- no user text is interpreted as trusted system/tool instructions;
- tool descriptions/schema are static trusted code, never assembled from UGC;
- state-changing tools are marked non-read-only and keep the human visibly in the loop;
- destructive/share/invite/message operations are auditable;
- honor cancellation/abort for long-running WebMCP calls;
- remote MCP follows current MCP authorization guidance and does not rely on client-supplied identity claims alone.

## Location / heatmap privacy
- store/share only coarse location explicitly consented by the user;
- exact GPS/address is not required for the pilot;
- location never affects structural score/ranking;
- match pins may show only the candidate's consented coarse location;
- any population/aggregate heatmap must suppress small buckets (configurable minimum-count threshold, default >=3) or use another documented anti-inference rule;
- hidden/revoked users never affect aggregate counts.

## Data lifecycle
Provide user-visible/server-enforced actions for:
- revoke discovery sharing;
- delete one session;
- leave/remove from collaboration workspace;
- delete messages/artifacts where policy permits;
- account data export;
- account deletion/anonymization for pilot data;
- documented retention policy for audit/security events and backups.

Revocation must remove live discoverability immediately; backup retention may be asynchronous but must be documented.

## Transport/storage protection
- HTTPS only in hosted environments;
- DB credentials/secrets only from deployment environment;
- managed database encryption-at-rest or equivalent documented storage encryption;
- backups encrypted/access-controlled;
- object/media storage private by default with short-lived authorized/signed access if used;
- restrictive CORS; no wildcard credentials;
- CSP/origin isolation compatible with WebMCP; `tools` permissions policy constrained to intended origin(s).

