ID: SCALE-001

Status: **BACKLOG — PRODUCTION ARCHITECTURE / PROMOTE BEFORE WORLD-CONTENT SCALE-UP**
Priority: **P1 foundation before large-world/content multiplication**
Category: Architecture / Runtime Performance / Stress Validation
Board authority: #33
Milestone relationship: #358 M4 foundation
Related: #364 PERF-003 cave transition latency, #78 RUNTIME-016 underground simulation activation

## Product decision
Underworld should not treat a subsystem as production-ready merely because a tiny demonstration works.

Core rule:
> **Design and validate against the expected scale envelope, not the toy case.**

A system that handles one tree/resource/enemy/cell but collapses when normal player behavior produces hundreds is not finished; it has only passed a prototype case.

The project prefers spending materially more implementation/planning time on durable ownership, bounded work and scalable runtime seams rather than repeatedly replacing short-lived prototypes.

## Prototype policy
Short-lived spikes are allowed for discovery, but they are disposable by default.

A prototype/spike may become production only after it is deliberately promoted through production contracts, tests and review. Do not let temporary implementation details silently become persistent architecture because they happened to work for a day.

## Required scale-envelope declaration
For world-facing/runtime systems, acceptance should identify the expected operating envelope where applicable:
- authored/generated object counts;
- simultaneously active/relevant object counts;
- resident chunk/cell counts;
- update/tick frequency;
- creation/destruction bursts;
- persistence/delta counts;
- cold-entry and warm-reentry costs;
- memory/residency expectations;
- worst-frame hitch expectations.

Do not freeze arbitrary universal numbers. Use realistic gameplay scenarios and profiling evidence to establish subsystem-specific budgets.

## Architecture principles
### Bounded relevance
Runtime cost should primarily follow the **active/relevant set**, not total world history.

Avoid whole-world per-frame scans. Prefer spatial partitioning, streaming, sleeping/deactivation, event-driven updates and deterministic deferred work where appropriate.

### Durable semantic state vs runtime instances
Generated/runtime Nodes must not become durable world truth merely because many instances exist.

Persistent state should remain compact semantic identity + deltas. Unloaded regions/entities reconstruct from accepted authored/generated truth plus deltas rather than serializing entire runtime trees.

### Burst safety
Normal player behavior can create bursts that dwarf authored fixtures. Systems must be tested for these cases before content quantity multiplies.

Examples:
- player plants many seeds and 200+ trees mature in the same broad window;
- many dropped/recoverable items exist after combat/harvesting;
- dense resource/enemy area enters activation radius at once;
- cave transition requests several cells while other gameplay systems remain active;
- Save/Continue reconstructs a world containing many durable deltas.

### Cross-system load
Passing isolated microbenchmarks is insufficient. Run mixed scenarios where streaming, AI/simulation, resources, vegetation, loot, persistence and presentation are active together, because contention/hitches often appear only under composition.

## Surface vegetation stress scenario
When planting/growth exists, prove representative cases such as:
1. 1 tree — correctness baseline;
2. ~50 trees — ordinary dense player area;
3. ~100 trees — heavy but plausible local area;
4. **200+ player-grown trees** — stress case;
5. clustered maturation/growth update burst;
6. leave/re-enter region;
7. Save & Quit -> Continue;
8. harvesting/removal/regrowth where supported.

Acceptance is not that every tree is always fully simulated. The architecture should keep inactive trees cheap and avoid O(total-world-trees) work every frame.

